Tremella cerebriformis  ;C.-J. Chen sp. nov. (Figs. 6-8);;Fructificatio gyrosa vel cerebriformis, lucida, gelatinosa, pallide lutea. Basidia 31-36 (-40) x 30-40 ?m, oblique septata. Basidiosporae globosae vel ellipsoidae, 17-23(-25) x 18-24 ?m. Hyphidia 2-4 ?m ø, muris tenuibus. ;mesenterica;Basidiocarps gyrose to cerebriform, lucid, soft gelatinous, 2-3 cm ø, slightly whitish yellow when fresh, drying pale yellow, flat on the substrate. ; ±globose, subglobose to ellipsoid, occasionally oval, 31-36(-40) x 30-40 ?m [Q=0.85-1.23(-1.33)], longitudinally or obliquely septate, 4-spored/ sterigmata up to 180 ?m in length, 5-8 ?m ø, apically swollen up to 12-14 ?m ø/ thin-walled, smooth. ;±globose, 17-23(-25) x 18-24 ?m [Q=0.94-1.04], smooth, hyaline, germinating by budding or repetition. ; absent. ; absent. ; absent. ; smooth, thin-walled, mostly 2-4 ?m ø, branched, often anastomosing/ new hyphae branching from clamps or below septate/ hyphidia and basidia derived from the same generative hyphae. ; hyphae clamped, 2-4(-6) ?m, thin- or very slightly thick-walled, gelatinous/ anastomoses abundant/ hymenia amphigenous. ; absent. ; rare, subglobose, clamped, haustorial hyphae short and rarely branched. ; on decayed branches of Fagaceae. ;CCJ 1142 ; from Latin, cerebriformis, brain like, referring to the shape of the basidiocarp. ; Taiwan, Taichung county, Tahsueh Shan Forest, ca. 2000-2300 m. ; NTU-TW-Mycological Herbarium, Dept. Botany, National Taiwan University, Taipei, Taiwan (holotype)/ Universität Tübingen, Herbarium of Lehrstuhl Spezielle Botanik, Auf der Morgenstelle 1, D-72076 Tübingen, Germany (isotype). ;Taiwan ;Taiwan, Taichung county, Tahsueh Shan Forest, leg. C.-J. Chen, 12. VII. 1995, CCJ 1142. 
Tremella mesenterica;Retz.: Fr. 1822 (Figs. 11-24) ;Fries, Syst. Myc. 2: 214.  ?=T. lutescens Pers.: Fries, Syst. Myc. 2: 213. ;;mesenterica;Basidiocarps large and conspicuous, ca. 5 cm ø, gelatinous, pustular when young, foliose when mature, lobes undulate-plicate/ pure yellow to yellowish orange/ pallid to whitish yellow under dark environment/ young basidiocarps orange, color reduced when basidiocarps are mature/ dry becoming pure yellow to yellowish orange. ; two types: type I, subglobose to broadly ellipsoid, 20-23(-25) x (17-) 18-22(-23) ?m [Q=(0.95-)1.12-1.31]/ type II, oval, (20-)24-31(-35) x (15-)16-20 ?m [Q=1.40-1.82(-2.19)]/ longitudinally or obliquely cruciate-septate, 4-spored/ sterigmata up to 105 ?m in length, 2-3 ?m ø, apically swollen up to 5-7 ?m. ; broadly ellipsoid to oval, (10-)12-15.5 x (7-)9-10(-12) ?m [Q=1.20-1.40(-1.61)], smooth, hyaline, germinating by repetition or budding. ;ellipsoid, fusiform to cylindrical, 3-5(-7) x 1.0-2.0 ?m [Q=1.5-3.3(-7)], smooth, hyaline, clamped/ orange in cluster. ;variable, sometimes bearing from hyphae, occasionally bearing from swollen vesicle-like cells, globose, subglobose to ellipsoid, (12-)14-21 x (9-)11-16.5(-19) ?m [Q=1.00-1.18(-1.42)]/ when ovate to clavate, mostly 22-27 x 15-17 ?m [Q=1.35-1.92]/ sometimes lanceolate (lance-shaped), mostly 34-35 x 7-10 ?m [Q=3.5-4.86]/ thick-walled, 2-3.5 ?m in thickness. ;constantly on basal parts of mature basidiocarps close to the substrate, big, clamped, colorless/ when citriniform, mostly 26-37 x (13-)16-22 ?m [Q=1.30-1.85(-2.19)]/ when broadly pyriform, 18-24 x 13-18 ?m (not including stalks) [Q= 1.14-1.40], stalks often 2-5 x 1-2 ?m, sometimes up to 16 ?m [if data including stalks, then mostly 20-39 x 13-18 ?m, Q=1.31-1.95]/ when subglobose to oval, (15-)21-29(-34) x (12-)17-26 ?m [Q=(1.00-)1.06-1.42(-1.61)]/ bearing on citriniform, broadly pyriform (mostly with short stalk) or subglobose to globose cells. Terminally swollen cells in the hymenium colorless, globose to ellipsoid, mostly 3-9 x 3-8 ?m [Q=1.00-1.23], sometimes oval, 7-10 x 4-5 ?m [Q=1.71-2.22]. Some terminally and laterally swollen cells in subhymenium and mostly in the inner part of basidiocarps, oval, 12-17 x 5-6 ?m [Q=2-3.4], smooth, thin- to mostly thick-walled. ; present but not abundant, 2-4 ?m ø/ thin- to slightly thick-walled, gelatinous. ;mostly 2-3 ?m ø, sometimes up to 4 ?m in the inner part of basidiocarps/ thin- to thick-walled, gelatinous/ clamps abundant, closed or medallion-like, sometimes loop-like forming a big hollow. ;; abundant in the inner part of basidiocarps close to bark, globose to oblong, 3-6(-7) x 2.5-4 ?m [Q=1.3-2.0(-2.3)], mostly with slender, long branched, tortuous hyphae. ; on decayed branches of Fagus sylvatica or Abies alba. ;;;Europe. ;; China, Europe, Japan, North America, Taiwan (Bandoni, 1957a/ Binyamini, 1983/ Bjørnekaer, 1944/ Bourdot, 1932/ Breitenbach & Kraenzlin, 1986/ Kobayasi, 1939/ Looney, 1933/ Lowy, 1955/ McIlvaine, 1900/ Neuhoff, 1931/ Olive, 1946/ Roberts, 1995/ Torkelsen, 1968/ Wojewoda, 1981). ; Germany, Baden-Württemberg: Schönbuch in Tübingen, leg. Z.-L. Yang, 26. V. 1996, CCJ 1543/ France, Wintzfelden, leg. C.-J. Chen, 10. V. 1997, CCJ 1542/ Germany, Baden-Württemberg: Schwarzwald, Wildenreute-Sexau, bei Emmendingen, leg. F. Oberwinkler, 02. IX. 1975, FO 23014/ Taiwan, Taichung county, Tahsueh Shan Forest, 2000-2300 m, leg. C.-J. Chen, 21. III. 1995, CCJ 1040 & CCJ 1041/ Germany, Baden-Württemberg, Tübingen, Schönbuch, leg. R. Kirschner, 6. IX. 1996, CCJ 1556. 
Tremella aurantia ;Schw.: Fr. 1822 (Figs. 116-121) ;Fries, Syst. Myc. 2: 213.  =Tremella aurantia Schw. Naturf. Ges. Leipzig Schrift. 1: 114. 1822.  =Naematelia quercina Coker, Jour. Mitch. Soc. 35: 135. 1920.  =Naematelia aurantia (Schw.) Burt, Ann. Missouri Bot. Gard. 8: 368. 1921. ;;aurantia;Basidiocarps yellow to bright orange, elongate when young, hemispherical when mature, almost foliaceous, the lobes rugose and plicate, larger than 1 cm ø, growing up from the surface or side of host basidiocarps/ dry yellowish brown, stout, firm. ;two types: type I, globose, subglobose, 10.5-13.0(-14.0) x 10.5-13.0 (-14.0) ?m [Q=(0.92-) 1.00-1.18]/ type II, pyriform to clavate, 10.5-15.5(-24) x 10.5-13.0(-14.0) ?m [Q=1.07-1.41(-2.09)] (including stalks)/ stalks mostly 2-3 ?m, up to 5 ?m in length, 2-3 ?m in width/ longitudinally or obliquely cruciate-septate, 4-spored/ sterigmata 2-3 ?m ø, up to 50 ?m in length, apically swollen up to 5 ?m ø. ;broadly ellipsoid to ovoid, 7-9 x 6-7 ?m [Q=(1.08-)1.14-1.33(-1.71)], hyaline, smooth, germinating by budding or repetition. ;monokaryotic, variable in shape, abundant, mostly subglobose to oval, 3-5 x 2.5-3.5 ?m [Q=1.15-1.67], occurring in young basidiocarps in the hymenium and subhymenium close to the substrate. ;absent ;two types: type I, mostly ovate to clavate, (9-)11-16 x 4-7(-8) ?m [Q=(1.38-) 1.57-3.56(-4.25)], type II, globose to subglobose, 6.5-12.5 x 6.5-11 ?m [Q=1.00-1.08], existing in the subhymenium and inner part of basidiocarps. ;intertwined in the hymenium, smooth, hyaline, mostly 2-3(-4) ?m ø, clamped. ;smooth, hyaline, mostly 1.5-3.0 ?m ø/ clamps abundant, sometimes clamps germinating to a hypha conjugating with another cell, or to be a clamped haustorium or young basidium/ hymenia amphigenous, loose. ;; mostly 3-5 x 2.5-4.5 ?m [Q=1.00-1.40], single hyphae, rarely branched. ;on Stereum hirsutum growing on wood of angiosperms. ;;;;North Carolina. ;North America, Canada, Europe (Bandoni, 1957a/ Breitenbach &  Kraenzlin, 1986/ Llowy, 1955/ Roberts, 1995/ Wojewoda, 1981). ;Canada, British Columbia, Squamish, leg. R. B. Bandoni, 26. IX. 1959, BC639. 
Tremella encephala; Pers. ex. Bref. 1888 (Figs. 128-134) Brefeld, Untersuch. 7: 127;=Naematelia encephala (Pers.) Fries, Syst. Myc. 2: 227. 1822. ;;aurantia;Basidiocarps globose to subglobose, larger than 1 cm ø/ in section, consisting of a fleshy-fibrous core surrounded by a gelatinous outer layer/ the top surface whitish, gelatinous, the base part close to the substrate yellowish white, dry pale yellow (specimen CCJ897) to brown (specimen CCJ925)/ inner nucleus on top white, inside yellowish white, compact, pink color when dry. ;two types: type I, mostly globose to subglobose, 13-16 x 12-16 ?m [Q=1.0-1.06]/ type II, broadly ellipsoid to pyriform, 19-23 x 12-14 ?m [Q=1.36-1.92]/ producing terminally or laterally, often in opposite pairs or alternately along the fertile hyphae, 2- or 4-spored, longitudinally or obliquely septate/ sterigmata 2-3 ?m ø, up to 65 ?m in length, mostly apically swollen, up to 4-4.5 (6) ?m ø, sometimes aciculate in soaked basidiocarps, up to 145 ?m in length. ;globose to subglobose, 6.5-10(-11) x 6-9(-10) ?m [Q=1-1.23], germinating by budding, repetition, or germ tubes on the surface of soaked basidiocarps. ;not seen. ;;abundant in the subhymenium and inner part of basidicarps, two types: type I, subglobose to globose , 6.5-9 x 6-8 ?m [Q=1.0-1.2]/ type II, ovate to ventricose, (18) 9-14 x 5-7 ?m [Q=1.42-2.80]/ thin-walled, mostly germinating with clamped hyphae. ;abundant in the hymenium close to the substrate, 2-5(-7) ?m ø, thin-walled. ;thick-walled, gelatinous, clamped, 2-4 ?m ø/ hymenia amphigenous. ;;abundant close to the core, rare in the subhymenium, clamped, haustorial hyphae rarely branched. ;mostly on coniferous wood, occasionally on angiosperm wood. ;;;Europe (Persoon did not mention the type locality).  ;;Europe, Japan, Taiwan (new record), and U.S.A. (Bandoni, 1957a/ Bjørnekaer, 1944/ Breitenbach & Kraenzlin, 1986/ Donk, 1969/ Jülich, 1983/ Neuhoff, 1935/ T;Taiwan, Taipei, Yangmingshan National Park, ca. 300m, on Stereum sanguinoletum on branches of Pinus taiwanesis, leg. C.-J. Chen, 17. II. 1994, CCJ 897/ Taiwan, Hsinchu county, Kuanwu Forest, 2000-2300 m, on Stereum sp. on branches of Pinus sp., leg. C.-J. Chen, 19. III. 1994, CCJ 925/ Germany, Baden-Württemberg, Tübingen, Rosenau, 460 m, leg. F. Oberwinkler, 02. X. 1984, FO 35974. 
Tremella giraffa;C.-J. Chen sp. nov. (Figs. 135-144) ;;Fungus fructificatione destitutus. Basidia subglobosa, ellipsoidea, pyriformia vel capitata, (9-)10-13(-16) x (7-)8-10(-11) ?m (sine stipite), longitudinaliter vel oblique septata, stipes usque ad 36 ?m. Sterigmata 18-35(-60) x 2-3 ?m, parte apicali usque ad 3.5-4.5 ?m ø. Basidiosporae subglobosae vel ellipsoideae, 7-8.5(-9.5) x (5-)6-7.5(-8.5) ?m. Hyphae 2-3(-4) ?m ø, muris tenuibus, pseudofibulis. Parasitus in Dacrymycete stillato. ;aurantia;Basidiocarp lacking. Mycoparasite in basidiocarps of Dacrymyces stillatus Nees: Fr. ;subglobose, ellipsoid, pyriform to capitate, (9-)10-13(-16) x (7-)8-10 (-11) ?m [Q=(0.91-)1.05-1.50] (measurements not including stalks), mostly with stalks/ stalks up to 36 ?m (especially the young basidia on the surface of host basidiocarps)/ longitudinally or obliquely septate, mostly 2-spored, rarely 1-spored/ sterigmata up to 60 ?m in length, mostly 18-35 x 2-3 ?m, apically swollen, 3.5-4.5 ?m ø/ frequently new basidia growing from basidia, two or three basidia in chains, occasionally one basidium producing two basidia instead of sterigmata. ;subglobose to broadly ellipsoid, 7-8.5(-9.5) x (5-)6-7.5(-8.5) ?m [Q=1.06-1.25(-1.5)], sometimes oval to fusiform on the surface of basidiocarp (Q value up to 1.80)/ germinating by budding or repetition, rarely by germ tubes, if by germ tubes, haustoria forming directly from septa. ;absent. ;absent. ;absent. ;absent. ; pseudoclamped, thin-walled or slightly thick-walled, gelatinous, hyaline, 1.5-2.5(-3.5) ?m ø. ;;pseudoclamped, abundant, growing from hyphae, sometimes from basidia, slender, tortuous hyphae attaching to host hyphae. ;in basidiocarps of Dacrymyces stillatus growing on branches of Larix decidua. ;CCJ 1553. ;giraffe animal with long neck-like stalked basidia. ; Germany, Tübingen, Schönbuch;Universität Tübingen, Herbarium of Lehrstuhl Spezielle Botanik, Auf der Morgenstelle 1, D-72076 Tübingen, Germany (holotype)/ NTU-TAI-Mycological Herbarium, Department of Botany, National Taiwan University, Taipei, Taiwan. R.O.C. (isotype). ;Germany. ;Germany, Tübingen, Schönbuch, leg. Roland Kirschner, 29. VI. 1997, CCJ 1553. 
Tremella foliacea ;Pers.: Fr. 1822 sensu lato (Figs. 84-92)  Fries, Syst. Myc. 2: 212. ;;;foliacea ;Basidiocarps foliose, lobes caespitose, cartilaginous, undulate, 2-3 cm ø, reddish brown to dark brown when fresh, becoming black when drying. ;two types: type I, ±globose to subglobose, 12-14(-16) x 11-15 ?m [Q=(0.80-)0.90-1.14(-1.17)]/ type II, ellipsoid to oval, 13-19(-22) x 11-14 ?m [Q=1.21-1.58(-2.05)], with very short stalks/ both longitudinally or obliquely septate, 4-spored/ mostly sterigmata 20-30 ?m, up to 50 ?m in length, 2-4 ?m ø, not or slightly apically swollen, up to 5 ?m ø/ often subhymenial hyphae anastomosing. ; subglobose to mostly broadly ellipsoid, 7-9 x 5-8 ?m [Q=(1.13-)1.17-1.31], smooth, hyaline/ germinating by repetition, budding, or germ tubes, secondary spores mostly subglobose. ;absent. ;absent. ;±globose to oval, 9-17 x 6-10 ?m [Q=(0.95-)1.00-2.00(-2.62)], smooth, hyaline, thin-walled, occasionally thick-walled. ;;smooth, hyaline, 2-4(-6) ?m ø, up to 10 ?m in subhymenium, mostly thick-walled, gelatinous, occasionally thin-walled in young stages, anastomoses frequent. ;; rare, clamped. ;on decayed Abies kawakamii. ;;;Europe, but no type locality mentioned by Persoon. ;;Europe, Japan, North America, North Africa, Australia, New Zealand, Taiwan (new record) (Bandoni, 1957a/ Bjørnekaer, 1944/ Breitenbach & Kraenzlin, 1986/ Donk, 1969/ Kobayasi, 1939/ Llody, 1920/ Looney, 1933/ Lowy, 1955/ Neuhoff, 1935/ Olive, 1947/ Reid & Austwick, 1963/ Torkelsen, 1968/ Wojewoda, 1981). ;Taiwan, Hsinchu county, Kuanwu Forest, 2200 m, leg. C.-J. Chen, 19. III. 1994, CCJ 914/ Taiwan, Taichung county, Tahsueh Shan Forest, leg. C.-J. Chen, 24. XI. 1996. CCJ 1396. 
Tremella griseobrunnea;nom. prov. (Figs. 96-97) ;;;foliacea ;Basidiocarps grayish brown to brownish black, caespitose, foliose, not lobe-like, amphigenous, a little bit undulate, ca. 2 cm ø, dry becoming black, horny. ;globose to oval, (10-)11-15 x (8-)9-11.5 ?m [Q=(0.95-)1.05-1.47 (-1.88)], rarely with short stalks, stalks not longer than 2 ?m/ longitudinally or obliquely septate, mostly 4-, occasionally 2- or 3-spored, sometimes septa irregular/ sterigmata mostly 15-25 ?m in length, 2-3 ?m ø, rarely apically swollen, up to 4 ?m ø/ mostly two terminal basidia together. ;subglobose to broadly ellipsoid, 6-7 x 5-6.5 ?m [Q=(1.00-)1.09-1.20 (-1.27)], smooth, hyaline, germinating by repetition or budding. ;absent. ; absent. ;absent. ;;smooth, hyaline, clamped, very slightly thick-walled, gelatinous, 1.5-4 ?m ø, frequently anastomosing, particularly in the subhymenium. ;; rare. ;on decayed wood. ; CCJ 1561. ;;Germany, Bayern, Oberjoch, Pfeiffermühle. ;at the Universität Tübingen, Herbarium of Lehrstuhl Spezielle Botanik, Auf der Morgenstelle 1, D-72076 Tübingen, Germany (holotype). ;Germany. ;Germany, Bayern, Oberjoch, Pfeiffermühle. leg. José Paulo Sampaio, 30. IX. 1997. CCJ 1561. 
Tremella mycophaga;Martin 1940 (Figs. 98-101)  Mycologia 32: 686. ;;;foliacea ;Basidiocarps growing on the hymenium of Aleurodiscus amorphus, minute, pulvinate, becoming confluent or caespitose up to 0.5-1.0 cm ø, soft gelatinous, transparent pale yellow to light brown, dry becoming brown. ;±subglobose to ellipsoid, sometimes oval, (13-)14-18(-30) x 12-17 ?m [Q=(0.81-)0.88-1.33(-2.31)], longitudinally or obliquely cruciate-septate, mostly 2- or 4-spored, occasionally 1- or 3-spored, sometimes septate irregular/ sterigmata up to 130 ?m in length, 2-3 ?m ø, rarely apically swollen up to 4 ?m ø. ;globose to subglobose, 7-9 x 6-9 ?m [Q=1.00-1.25], smooth, hyaline/ germinating by repetition, budding or sometimes by germ tubes. ;two types: type I, dikaryotic with clamps, abundant in young basidiocarps, shapes variable, 4-7 x 2.5-4.5 ?m [Q=1.22-2.16(-2.60)], thin-walled, hyaline/ conidiogenous cells mostly 7-15 x 3-5 ?m [Q=1.6-2.57 (-4.29)], producing conidia with clamps, generally each conidiogenous cell producing three conidia/ type II, monokaryotic conidia budding from vase-like conidiogenous cells, subglobose to oval, 2-3 x 1.5-2 ?m [Q=1.07-1.67]/ conidiogenous cells fusiform, tapered apically, 6-9 x 3.5-5 ?m. ;absent. ;absent. ;;hyaline, smooth, 1.5-2.0(-3.0) ?m ø, swollen up to 5 ?m ø. ;absent ; rare. ;on Aleurodiscus amorphus growing on Abies spp. ;;;Ontario, Algonquin Park. ;;North America, Canada, Japan, Europe (Bandoni, 1957a/ Dueñas, 1996/ Jülich, 1983/ Martin, 1940/ Pilát, 1953/ Torkelsen, 1968/ Wojewoda,1981/ Zugmaier et al., 1994). ;Germany, Baden-Württemberg, Altenheim, Tannenwald, leg. F. Oberwinkler, 3. V. 1979, FO 28255a/ Germany, Baden-Württemberg, Tübingen, Heuberger Tor, leg. R. Kirschner, 1. V. 1996, CCJ1539. 
Tremella vasifera ;C.-J. Chen sp. nov. (Figs. 111-115) ;;Fructificatio foliacea, gelatinosa, rubello-brunnea vel brunnea. Basidia subglobosa ad pyriformia, 12-15 x 9.5-12.5 ?m, oblique septata. Basidiosporae ellipsoideae vel ovales, 6.5-8(-9) x 4.5-6.5 ?m. Conidia 3-5.5(-7) x (1.5-)2-3(-4) ?m. Conidiophora (6.5-)8-11(-13) x (5-)6-8.5(-10) ?m, muris crassis. ;foliacea ;Basidiocarps large, foliose, more than 5 cm ø, reddish brown to brown when fresh, dry dark brown to black. ;subglobose, short-stalked to pyriform, 12-15 x 9.5-12.5 ?m [Q=1.08-1.30] (not including stalks), longitudinally or obliquely cruciate-septate, 2- to 4-spored/ sterigmata mostly up to 45 ?m, 1.5-2.5 ?m ø, apically swollen up to 4 ?m ø. ;ellipsoid to oval, 6.5-8(-9) x 4.5-6.5 ?m [Q=1.17-1.44], smooth, hyaline, germinating by budding, repetition, or sometimes germ tubes. ;smooth, hyaline, 3-5.5(-7) x (1.5-)2-3(-4) ?m [Q=1.50-2.00], budding from vessel-like conidiogenous cells/ conidiogenous cells (6.5-)8-11 (-13) x (5-)6-8.5(-10) ?m [Q=(1.00-)1.10-1.53(-1.67)], thick-walled, abundant, occasionally mixed with basidia in the hymenium. ;;subterminally and terminally swollen cells, globose to subglobose, sometimes oval, (10-)12-18(-24) x (10-)12-15 ?m [Q=1.0-1.23(-1.60)], located in the inner and basal parts of basidiocarps close to the substrate, color yellow in 5% KOH solution/ some strongly swollen, terminal and subterminal cells present on the sterile surface of the basidiocarp close to the substrate, mostly globose, rarely oval, 20-35 x 20-31 ?m [Q=1.0-1.59], frequently thin-, sometimes thick-walled, smooth. ;; hyaline, 2.5-7.0 ?m ø, thin- to thick-walled, gelatinous. ;; rare, haustorial hyphae normally single, occasionally forked. ;on Alnus incana. ;FO 38280 ; from Latin vas, vessel, referring to the shape of conidiogenous cells. ;Germany, Bayern, Allgäu: from Oberjoch through Trettachtal to Spielmannsau, 800-1000 m. ;Universität Tübingen, Herbarium of Lehrstuhl Spezielle Botanik, Auf der Morgenstelle 1, D-72076 Tübingen, Germany (holotype). ;Germany. ;Germany, Bayern, Allgäu: from Oberjoch through Trettachtal to Spielmannsau, 800-1000 m, leg. F. Oberwinkler, 12. IX. 1987, FO
